                        \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhen $k=3,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(2)-\\frac{3}{2}\\ln(3)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhen $k=4,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332687 1113 #uuid "3c40477e-0e07-54d1-aab1-cee420dae851" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332687 1145 "When $k=4,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+4}=\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =A(x+2)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to solve for $A$ and $B,$ and both of these constants appear in the equation simultaneously. However, we can eliminate $A$ if we substitute $x=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2-2 & =A(-2+2)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve for $A.$ To do this, let’s start by substituting our value $B=-4$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =A(x+2)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute some value for $x$ and solve for $A.$\n\nWe *can’t* choose $x=-2,$ since this would eliminate $A$ from the equation. But we can choose any other value of $x.$ So let’s choose a simple value, say, $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-2 & =A(0+2)-4 \\\\ -2 & =2A-4 \\\\ 2 & =2A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332688 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332689 13194139535129 true] [17592186332688 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535129 true] [17592186332688 1041 "field-1" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332688 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332689 13194139535129 true] [17592186332688 1110 #uuid "6079467c-1276-52dc-a012-340c0c2f69b9" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332689 1034 "1" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535129 true] [17592186332689 1111 #uuid "d9896aca-5c9e-599e-98ed-ad9398ac2ecf" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332690 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332691 13194139535129 true] [17592186332690 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535129 true] [17592186332690 1041 "field-2" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332690 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332691 13194139535129 true] [17592186332690 1110 #uuid "c3f4efb9-1648-530f-9c89-5466d9dc2969" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332691 1034 "-4" 13194139535129 true] [17592186332691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535129 true] [